  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Monsoon moisture drives thunderstorms through Thursday.\n\n- Hot, dry, breezy conditions this weekend are a concern for fire weather.\n\n- Smoke from Pacific Northwest wildfire will continue to impact\n  central Idaho and western Montana. \n\nThe Southwest Monsoon is in full force and keeps reinforcing the\nsubtropical ridge over the western U.S and injecting moisture into\nit. For the next few days, that moisture will work its way into\nsouthern Idaho and all of Lemhi County in Idaho as well as\nsouthwest Montana. The result is thunderstorms in these regions.\nThe amount of water that they have to work with and the relatively\nweak steering flow under the ridge make heavy rain and flash\nfloods the primary risks from these storms. \n\nLate Thursday and Friday a broad trough over the northeast Pacific\nOcean pushes the ridge further east, bringing in much drier air.\nThe drier air and breezy conditions are a major concern for fire\nweather. Though we haven't seen much local impact from wildland\nfire despite a lot of recent lightning, the hot, dry, breezy\nconditions this weekend are exactly the kind of weather that will\npush the landscape to be more receptive to fire. \n\nWhile our region hasn't seen much impact from wildland fire yet,\nthe Pacific Northwest has in eastern Oregon and eastern\nWashington. Fires in these areas have been producing quite a bit\nof smoke and pushing it towards our region. As the broad Pacific\ntrough pushes the ridge to the east this weekend, that will open\nthe door for more of that smoke to make into central Idaho and\nwestern Montana. We'll be watching that situation closely for air\nquality impacts.",
